{
 "source": "PC NFH2, the anger gauge's jumps in Badinfos' 100 % run (tools/pcref/amounts.py: the jump per second scaled to the full bar, 92 % of the crop, labelled with the HUD bubble's activity) — a jump that hits the full bar is a lower bound and keeps the mobile value where that is higher; a jump shared by linked coins is split in the mobile proportions; an unmeasured coin keeps the mobile value. E05 Ping-Pong Fucius: +25 the tennis (124 s), +38 the skis (154), +51 the chef (192; the eels item carries no anger of its own — the port's meter rose by the Chef's amount alone), +33 the rockets at the full bar (228), the sculpture +2 on the full bar (245). PC data (cn_b2/tricks.xml, rage per trick in thousandths of the gauge): the mobile AngerAmount values are the PC's for every trick this level pays — no overlay. The gauge jumps read off Badinfos' video (2026-09-08) ran 10-25 %% high and are withdrawn. The gauge is 100 000 rage long — the mobile's AngryMeterMaximum 100: the PC dialog (nfh2 dialogs/*/menuleft_bar.xml) gives the rageometer range 0..100000 and GameLogic.dll flags 100000 (docs/PC_ROUTINES.md); the 80 read off the video was the bar's geometry, as the Season 1 tube's was. Its decay is the mobile's 0.37 %/s: the bar falls 41 -> 11 % over 70 trick-free seconds of E01 (tools/pcref/gauge.py, x0.8 for the crop). The decay is leveldata.xml's time=\"30\" per 1/12 s tick (GameLogic.dll's level update 0x100442b3, 0x100447c4: rage -= the level record's +0x28, the app's leveldata parser stores `time` there; the HUD clock divides the tick count by 12) — PCRageDecay 30, 0.36 %/s against the mobile's 0.37. The reactions (tools/pcref/coins.py --write-laugh): PCLaugh = the `<trick laugh=>` level of the records the item's trick fires, which picks the PC neighbour's reaction clip (pcprofile.S2_REACTION_CLIPS). The door passes (tools/pcref/pc_walks_s2.py): PCPass on each Transition = per pawn the PC pass of the door pair it stands for — the near door's <actor>_in against the room's floor, the straight movement to the far door's <actor>_out (or the enter + leave ticks), the far floor against <actor>_out, the x of both hotspots, in px of the PC scene; PCApproach on the actors' stations and Woody's items = per role the PC object's <actor> hotspot x and its height against the room's floor (tx: the neighbour's move over the station's actions, their <translation>s; txt: after a tricked visit); PCRoom on each Zone = its PC room's floor line and <neighbor> records with the doors' <actor> hotspots, for the path finder's routes. Station durations (tools/pcref/pc_durations_s2.py): GameLogic.dll's level script (tools/pcref/lap_model_s2.py code_stays: the untricked lap's actions, hideouts and bars per mobile item), the PC video bubble spans of docs/PC_LAPS_DETAIL.md less the walk for the items the model leaves untimed, PCUseSeconds per visit. An item's own clips while another role uses it hidden (tools/pcref/pc_durations_others.py ITEM_CLIPS): PCItemClipSeconds, the level data's actions and clips paired by hand. The mini-game (tools/pcref/pc_minigames.py): PCMinigameTicks = the `time` of the game object's Woody action in objects.xml, PCMinigameLevels = the startlevel and endlevel of its combine.xml combination (the wobble's range), PCMinigameFailed = the behavioractor of its `failed` action (the actor a lost game sends running), PCMinigameFailedTicks = that action's ticks from its job's first update to the one that posts its behaviour (the Loader's time + 2), PCMinigameFailedClip = the clip that actor's behaviour plays first when it is not the neighbour (203's Olga shouts, 69 frames, and her shout's own behaviour, his run, is posted as her shout's job ends, its `ticks`), PCMinigameLift = the height in level px of Woody's place for the game (the object's `woody` hotspot) above its room's floor line, the field's middle 150 px above it.",
